# spawn_basic.tkr — the §10 spawn batch end-to-end fixture (C backend). It proves a working
# `spawn f(args)`: fire-and-forget concurrent execution of a call whose arguments crossed the thread
# boundary BY COPY. Four workers are spawned, each on its own detached thread and sub-root arena; each
# writes an observable per-tag file whose NAME derives from its `i64` tag and whose CONTENT is its
# deep-copied `str` argument. The main thread clears the files first (so only a thread that actually
# ran can satisfy the poll), spawns the four, then bounded-polls each file for its EXACT expected
# content. A correct filename proves the scalar tag survived the copy; exact content proves the `str`
# was deep-copied intact (a dangling spawning-arena pointer would read back as garbage). All four
# observed with correct per-tag content -> exit 0; any missing/corrupt -> exit 1.

Feature: spawn_basic — a working `spawn f(args)` runs a detached thread with args copied across the boundary

  Scenario: four_workers_each_write_their_copied_args — every spawned thread ran and its args were intact
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
